---
import Lab from '../../layouts/Lab.astro';
import Term from '../../components/Term.astro';
import MaxSat from '../../islands/MaxSat';

const sections = [
  { id: 'plain-words', label: 'In plain words' },
  { id: 'solver', label: 'Figure 1: run the solver' },
  { id: 'how', label: 'How it works' },
  { id: 'trade-offs', label: 'Decisions and trade-offs' },
  { id: 'limits', label: 'Limits and next steps' },
  { id: 'real', label: 'What is real here' },
];
---

<Lab
  slug="maxsat"
  title="MaxSAT solver"
  context="MSc coursework, Evolutionary Computation, 2025"
  summary="An evolutionary search with a local-search boost, solving benchmark problems in your browser."
  sections={sections}
  facts={[
    { label: 'Role', value: 'Sole author' },
    { label: 'When', value: '2025. Module mark 97 out of 100' },
    { label: 'Tools', value: 'Python' },
    {
      label: 'Repository',
      value: 'github.com/Harsh-159/evolutionary-maxsat-solver',
      href: 'https://github.com/Harsh-159/evolutionary-maxsat-solver',
    },
  ]}
>
  <section id="plain-words" aria-labelledby="plain-words-h">
    <h2 id="plain-words-h">In plain words</h2>
    <p>
      Some problems are long lists of conditions that cannot all be true at once. <Term id="maxsat">MaxSAT</Term> asks for
      the answer that satisfies as many as possible. Trying every answer is hopeless: 147 yes-or-no variables give about 10
      to the power 44 combinations. This solver keeps a population of candidate answers, breeds and mutates the better ones,
      and gives some of them a quick polish by local search, which makes it a <Term id="memetic">memetic algorithm</Term>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="figure-section"><MaxSat client:visible /></section>

  <section id="how" aria-labelledby="how-h">
    <h2 id="how-h">How it works</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Start with random answers: one true-or-false value per variable for each member of the population.</li>
      <li>Choose each parent by tournament: draw 5 members at random and keep the best.</li>
      <li>Cross two parents: each variable comes from one parent or the other with equal chance.</li>
      <li>Mutate the child: flip each variable with probability 1/n, so about one flip per child.</li>
      <li>
        For 15% of children, run local search: scan the variables in random order and flip any that satisfies more clauses.
        Stop when a full scan finds no improvement, or after flips equal to half the number of variables.
      </li>
      <li>Carry the best member into the next generation unchanged.</li>
      <li>Repeat until the budget is spent.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Fitness is kept up to date incrementally. Each clause has a count of how many of its literals are true, and each
      variable has an index of the clauses it appears in. The gain from flipping one variable is read from those two
      structures without re-reading the formula.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="trade-offs" aria-labelledby="trade-offs-h">
    <h2 id="trade-offs-h">Decisions and trade-offs</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Local search on 15% of children, not all. It exploits hard, and using it everywhere would leave too little exploration.</li>
      <li>Mutation at 1/n, which gives one expected flip per child.</li>
      <li>Incremental fitness over full re-evaluation, so a flip costs only the clauses that variable touches.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="limits" aria-labelledby="limits-h">
    <h2 id="limits-h">Limits and what I would do next</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>It solves unweighted MaxSAT only. Every clause counts the same, as the coursework specified.</li>
      <li>This page uses a generation budget. The original uses a time budget.</li>
      <li>There is no comparison with exact solvers.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="real" aria-labelledby="real-h">
    <h2 id="real-h">What is real here</h2>
    <p class="real-box">
      The algorithm and its default settings are those of my coursework solver. The code running on this page is a
      JavaScript port of it. The four problems come from the MaxSAT Evaluation 2017 benchmark set.
    </p>
  </section>
</Lab>
